BEGIN:VCALENDAR
VERSION:2.0
PRODID:-//Littoralicious//Terroir Kendwa//EN
X-WR-CALNAME:Kendwa — eat & drink checklist
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-1@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Pweza wa nazi — Octopus simmered slowly in coconut curry until it gives.
DESCRIPTION:Say: PWEH-za wa NA-zi. The plate the Swahili coast is known for. The tell of a real one is that the sauce has cooked long enough for the coconut oil to break out and shine on top — pour-over versions never split. Best: Machnoo\, Nungwi village
LOCATION:Machnoo\, Nungwi village
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Machnoo+Restaurant+Nungwi+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-2@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Urojo — Sour turmeric-yellow soup of mango and tamarind\, assembled at the counter with bhajia\, potato and cassava crisps.
DESCRIPTION:Say: oo-ROH-jo. Often called Zanzibar mix and unofficially Tanzania's national dish\; the record puts its origin in Dar es Salaam\, not here. Judge it on the sourness — tamarind and lime\, never vinegar. Best: Forodhani Gardens\, Stone Town
LOCATION:Forodhani Gardens\, Stone Town
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Forodhani+Gardens+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-3@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Zanzibar pizza — Thin dough on an oiled griddle\, filled\, an egg cracked in\, folded into a parcel and fried both sides.
DESCRIPTION:Say: as it looks. Neither Italian nor traditional — invented at the Forodhani stalls and now the island's most recognisable street food. The one rule: it must be made in front of you\, never taken off a tray. Best: Forodhani Gardens night market\, from 18:00
LOCATION:Forodhani Gardens night market\, from 18:00
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Forodhani+Gardens+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-4@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Mchuzi wa nazi — The coconut mother sauce — grated coconut pressed twice\, built with garlic\, ginger\, turmeric\, cumin and tomato.
DESCRIPTION:Say: m-CHOO-zi wa NA-zi. Everything else on this island is a variation on it. Learn what a split\, oiled surface looks like and you can grade any kitchen here in one glance. Best: Kivulini Garden Lodge\, Nungwi
LOCATION:Kivulini Garden Lodge\, Nungwi
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Kivulini+Garden+Lodge+Nungwi+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-5@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Pilau — Rice cooked *in* the spiced stock — clove\, cumin\, cardamom\, black pepper — not sprinkled with spice after.
DESCRIPTION:Say: pee-LAU. The difference between pilau and 'spiced rice' is that the grain is cooked in the flavour\, so every grain is brown through. On an island that grew nine-tenths of the world's cloves\, this is the local proof. Best: Lukmaan\, Stone Town
LOCATION:Lukmaan\, Stone Town
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Lukmaan+Restaurant+Stone+Town+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-6@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Biryani — Layered rice and meat or fish\, cooked separately then assembled and steamed together.
DESCRIPTION:Say: beer-YA-nee. Zanzibar's Indian community has been cooking here since the dhow trade — biryani is not an import on this island\, it is a native cuisine with a sea route attached. Best: Lukmaan\, Stone Town
LOCATION:Lukmaan\, Stone Town
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Lukmaan+Restaurant+Stone+Town+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-7@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Mkate wa kumimina — 'The bread that is poured' — fermented rice-and-coconut batter baked over coals with heat above and below.
DESCRIPTION:Say: m-KA-teh wa koo-mee-MEE-na. Rice\, coconut and time\, with no wheat and no oven: the clearest single statement of what this island's cooking actually is. A household bread\, so you have to catch it. Best: Lukmaan's counter when it is on\, and Nungwi village kitchens
LOCATION:Lukmaan's counter when it is on\, and Nungwi village kitchens
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Lukmaan+Restaurant+Stone+Town+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-8@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Mishkaki — Marinated beef or goat on skewers\, grilled hard over charcoal.
DESCRIPTION:Say: mish-KA-kee. The evening street protein of the whole Swahili coast. Ask for it with pilipili and a squeeze of lime\; the marinade should be sour\, not sweet. Best: Forodhani\, and the Nungwi fish-market grills
LOCATION:Forodhani\, and the Nungwi fish-market grills
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Nungwi+market+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-9@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Nguru wa kuchoma — Whole grilled kingfish\, scored and turned over charcoal.
DESCRIPTION:Say: n-GOO-roo wa koo-CHO-ma. Kingfish is the north tip's standard catch. Buy it off the ice at the market\, agree the price per kilogram before it goes on the coals\, and ask for it butterflied. Best: Nungwi fish market\, from 18:00
LOCATION:Nungwi fish market\, from 18:00
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Nungwi+market+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-10@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Wali wa nazi — Rice cooked in coconut milk rather than water.
DESCRIPTION:Say: WA-li wa NA-zi. The default starch here and the reason a plain-looking plate of white rice tastes of something. Refuse the plain-boiled version if you are offered a choice. Best: Any village kitchen in Nungwi
LOCATION:Any village kitchen in Nungwi
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Nungwi+village+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-11@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Kachumbari — Raw tomato\, onion\, chilli and lime — the salad that comes with everything grilled.
DESCRIPTION:Say: ka-choom-BA-ri. It is not a garnish\, it is the acid the plate is built around\; the fat of the coconut sauce needs it. Onion should be rinsed\, never raw-harsh. Best: With every grill on the beach
LOCATION:With every grill on the beach
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Nungwi+market+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-12@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Chipsi mayai — A chip omelette — fries folded into beaten egg and set in a pan like a frittata.
DESCRIPTION:Say: CHIP-si ma-YAI. Tanzania's own late-night dish\, sold from carts and small kitchens everywhere. Cheap\, and better than it sounds when the egg is only just set. Best: Nungwi village dukas and the fish-market grills
LOCATION:Nungwi village dukas and the fish-market grills
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Nungwi+village+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-13@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Mandazi — Lightly sweet fried dough\, often with cardamom and coconut milk in the batter.
DESCRIPTION:Say: man-DA-zi. The coast's breakfast\, eaten with tea. Good ones are hollow and dry inside\; heavy\, oily ones are yesterday's. Best: Village dukas in Nungwi from early morning
LOCATION:Village dukas in Nungwi from early morning
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Nungwi+village+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-14@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Vitumbua — Small rice-and-coconut cakes cooked in a dimpled pan\, crisp outside and custardy in the middle.
DESCRIPTION:Say: vee-toom-BOO-a. Fermented rice batter again — the same technology as mkate wa kumimina\, in miniature and with sugar. Morning food\, gone by ten. Best: Nungwi village and Darajani market\, mornings
LOCATION:Nungwi village and Darajani market\, mornings
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Darajani+Market+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-15@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Kahawa chungu — Bitter black coffee\, spiced with cardamom and clove\, kept hot in a brass kettle over charcoal.
DESCRIPTION:Say: ka-HA-wa CHOON-goo. No sugar\, no milk\, small handleless cup\, drunk standing and handed back. On this coast it is the furniture of conversation rather than a caffeine delivery system. Best: The coffee men at the edges of Darajani\, Stone Town
LOCATION:The coffee men at the edges of Darajani\, Stone Town
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Darajani+Market+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-16@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Chai ya tangawizi — Black tea boiled hard with fresh ginger\, and usually cardamom and cinnamon.
DESCRIPTION:Say: chai ya tan-ga-WEE-zi. Ginger — tangawizi — is grown here\, and the tea is boiled with it rather than infused\, which is why it bites. The default hot drink of the village. Best: Any duka or village kitchen
LOCATION:Any duka or village kitchen
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Nungwi+village+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-17@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Madafu — A green\, immature coconut\, topped with a machete and drunk from the husk.
DESCRIPTION:Say: ma-DA-foo. The coast's original soft drink\, and free of the cold chain entirely. When you have finished\, hand it back and ask for the shell to be split so you can eat the soft flesh with a sliver of husk as a spoon. Best: Kendwa and Nungwi beaches\, from any vendor with a panga
LOCATION:Kendwa and Nungwi beaches\, from any vendor with a panga
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Kendwa+Beach+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-18@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Miwa — Sugar-cane juice\, pressed through a hand mill with lime and a knob of ginger.
DESCRIPTION:Say: MEE-wa. Pressed in front of you or not at all. The ginger is what stops it being sickly\; watch that the mill and the glass have been rinsed. Best: Forodhani Gardens and Darajani
LOCATION:Forodhani Gardens and Darajani
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Forodhani+Gardens+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-19@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Konyagi — Tanzania's sugar-cane spirit\, drunk long with tonic and lime.
DESCRIPTION:Say: kon-YA-gee. Sold as 'the spirit of the nation' and the local answer to gin. Have it once\, at a beach bar\, and note it is stronger than it drinks. Best: Cholo's Bar\, Nungwi
LOCATION:Cholo's Bar\, Nungwi
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Cholo%27s+Bar+Nungwi+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:kendwa-liste-20@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Karafuu — Cloves — the unopened flower bud\, hand-picked and dried until it rattles. The thing to take home.
DESCRIPTION:Say: ka-ra-FOO. Sultan Said's 1840s decree of three clove trees for every coconut palm made this archipelago the source of roughly nine-tenths of the world's cloves. Buy them loose by weight\, never in a printed box: a fresh bud is oily and marks the paper. Best: Darajani market\, Stone Town — loose\, by weight
LOCATION:Darajani market\, Stone Town — loose\, by weight
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Darajani+Market+Zanzibar
END:VTODO
END:VCALENDAR
